summary:
Improves Li–Liu's 2021 theorems on Chow groups and L-derivatives for unitary groups in two directions.
- Ramified places of E/F are allowed, with K_{r,v}-spherical representations there. This needs a Kudla–Rapoport-type formula Int(𝐋) = ∂Den(𝐋) on exotic smooth relative Rapoport–Zink spaces (Theorem 2.7).
- Arbitrary components at split places are allowed. This needs a vanishing theorem H^{2r}(X_m, Q_ℓ(r))_𝔪 = 0 for integral models with Drinfeld level, proved through Newton strata and the Caraiani–Scholze trace formula (Theorem 4.21).

The outcomes:
- Theorem 1.4: if L′(½, π) ≠ 0, the π-localized Chow group of the unitary Shimura variety is nonzero.
- Theorem 1.5: under the modularity of Kudla's generating functions, the arithmetic inner product formula relates the Beilinson–Bloch height of arithmetic theta lifts to L′(½, π) and local doubling zeta integrals.
- Corollary 1.7: an explicit formula for new vectors.
- Example 1.10: symmetric powers of elliptic curves now qualify.

The proof of Theorem 2.7 follows Li–Zhang, with four new ingredients:
- a parity-constrained lattice induction;
- a direct proof of the analytic vanishing (Proposition 2.22);
- a reduction to n = 4 computed by Howard's linear invariance;
- a display-theoretic comparison of relative and absolute Rapoport–Zink spaces when F/Q_p is unramified.

The libraries have none of this; the atlas plans the hermitian lattice classification (GN.2), the Igusa trace formula (IG.5) and Weil II purity (R34.5).

Routes:
- exotic Rapoport–Zink spaces and integral models go to UnitaryRapoportZinkSpacesAndRSZModels;
- the ramified Kudla–Rapoport theory goes to UnitaryKudlaRapoportCycles (both candidates from PAPER-LI-ZHANG-22-B);
- the doubling method and the arithmetic inner product formula go to a new Part II of GrossZagierAndArithmeticHeights;
- ramified local densities go to GN.3, the Weil representation to MP.2/MP.3, and the vanishing theorem to IG.5/IG.7, all as sources.

Three groups of misprints are recorded; no mathematical error was found.

routes.brief:
This reuses the candidate proposed by PAPER-LI-ZHANG-22-B, whose brief covers unramified unitary Rapoport–Zink spaces, the RSZ models and p-adic uniformization. Li–Liu, Forum Math. Pi 10 (2022), e5, add the ramified, exotic-good-reduction case. Keep this Part II moduli-theoretic, so that it does not depend on the cycle roadmaps.

Import the same owners as that brief (PELModuli M1–M5; AbelianSchemesAndArithmeticModuli A1–A4; FiniteFlatGroupsAndIntegralPadicHodgeTheory R07.1–R07.2, R07.4, R07.6; ShimuraVarieties V1, V4–V6), together with display theory (Zink 2002, Lau 2008) and O_F-displays (Ahsendorf–Cheng–Zink 2016).

Construct:
- the categories Exo_{(n−1,1)} (Kottwitz, wedge and spin conditions, ker λ = X[ι(u)], equivalently a symmetrization) and Exo_{(n,0)};
- the relative space N, formally smooth of relative dimension n − 1 with two components (RZ96, RSZ17 Prop. 3.10, Wu Thm. 5.18);
- the nonsplit space 𝐕 with the u^{−2}-scaled form;
- the absolute categories Exo^Φ (with the banal condition) for F/Q_p unramified;
- the admissible CM types and V♥;
- the auxiliary CM moduli Y (finite étale by Howard 2012).

The final theorems:
- Proposition 2.62: −^rel : Exo^{Φ,b} → Exo^b is an isomorphism of prestacks, built from the units ε_i, μ_u of (2.21) and Lemmas 2.60, 2.63.
- Corollaries 2.65–2.66: N^Φ ≅ N, and 𝐕 ≅ 𝐕^rel as hermitian spaces.
- The models X_m (Drinfeld level m at a split place; regular, flat, smooth for m = 0, projective; RSZ20 Thm. 4.5) and X_L̃ (smooth projective, exotic at the ramified place; RSZ20 Thm. 4.7, AT type (2)).
- Proposition 4.30, first part: Υ^rel : X^∧_0 → U(𝐕)(F)\[N × U(𝐕)(A^{∞,u}_F)/Π 𝐋_v] is an isomorphism.

Keep F/Q_p unramified, as the source does; Remark 2.68 leaves ramified F/Q_p open. Wu 2016 is stated for F = Q_p, and footnote 8 extends it; read it before relying on the general-F statement.

Tests: f = 1 (−^rel is the identity); n = 2; and the two connected components interchanged by an element of determinant −1.

This reuses the candidate proposed by PAPER-LI-ZHANG-22-B (unramified local and global Kudla–Rapoport theory and the arithmetic Siegel–Weil formula). It adds the ramified analogue of Li–Liu, Forum Math. Pi 10 (2022), e5, §2.

Import:
- the exotic Rapoport–Zink spaces N, N^Φ and 𝐕 from UnitaryRapoportZinkSpacesAndRSZModels;
- ramified hermitian local densities from GeometryOfNumbersAndQuadraticArithmetic GN.2–GN.3;
- Fourier analysis from MetaplecticAutomorphicForms MP.2;
- the Deligne–Lusztig and K-theory infrastructure already required by the unramified part.

Construct:
- the special divisors N(x), N(X) and Int(𝐋), and ♭(𝐕);
- ∂Den_{L^♭} with its horizontal and vertical parts (Definition 2.20);
- the strata V_Λ with the quadratic space Λ^∨/Λ;
- the complexes C(x), C(0) and the line bundle ω = L_X^{−1} of Howard's method;
- K_N(X)^{h,v} and Int^{h,v}_{L^♭};
- the cancellation law.

The final theorems, stated as the source does:
- Theorem 2.7: Int(𝐋) = ∂Den(𝐋) for every O_E-lattice of the nonsplit 𝐕 of even dimension n = 2r, E/F ramified, p odd.
- The steps to it:
  - Proposition 2.22, proved directly via Lemma 2.24;
  - Proposition 2.28 (Wu) and Corollaries 2.30–2.31;
  - Lemma 2.32 (Lusztig case ²D_n);
  - Proposition 2.33 with Corollaries 2.34–2.35 (linear invariance);
  - Lemma 2.40 (relative Cartier) and Lemma 2.42 (deg ω = q − 1 on each component of a type-4 stratum);
  - Proposition 2.44 (r = 1, via Keating's formula);
  - Lemmas 2.53–2.56 and Proposition 2.51.
- Corollary 2.66: N^Φ(x) = N(x^rel).
- The Whittaker comparison of Remark 2.18.

Keep the parity constraints explicit: n even, types and valuations of the parity of n. The induction reduces to n = 4, not n = 3 as in the unramified case. Apply sourceIssues E1–E2:
- (b_2 − j) in (2.17);
- θ(x_i) = x_i in Lemma 2.56.

Tests:
- r = 1 against Lemma 2.43;
- the values of Int_{V^+_Λ} in Lemma 2.55 and its Fourier transform;
- Int^+ = Int^−.

Start beyond Gross–Zagier formulas and arithmetic heights (GrossZagierAndArithmeticHeights). Its GZ.1 point heights, GZ.5 coherent theta kernels and GZ.6 arithmetic generating series with incoherent Eisenstein kernels are the rank-one prototype. This Part II proves the higher-rank analogue, the arithmetic inner product formula for U(r, r), in the form of Li–Liu, Ann. of Math. 194 (2021), 817–901, and its sequel Forum Math. Pi 10 (2022), e5.

Import:
- UnitaryKudlaRapoportCycles (Theorem 2.7 above and the unramified local Kudla–Rapoport theorem) and UnitaryRapoportZinkSpacesAndRSZModels (models and uniformization);
- MetaplecticAutomorphicForms MP.3–MP.6 (Weil representations, local theta correspondence, spherical modules);
- AutomorphicSpectralTheory AS.1–AS.2 (Eisenstein series, extended to Siegel degenerate principal series);
- IgusaVarietiesAndTorsionConcentration (Theorem 4.21);
- SmoothRepresentationsOfLocalGroups SR.2–SR.4, extended to the special maximal compact subgroups of ramified unitary groups.

Construct:
- the ramified spherical Hecke algebras and unramified principal series;
- the doubling zeta integrals with the section f^{(s)}_r, and the doubling L- and ε-factors (Yamana);
- the global notation of §4.1: R, S, Λ^R, the Hecke algebras T^R ⊇ S^R, and nearby spaces;
- Kudla's special cycles and generating functions in Chow groups;
- the arithmetic theta lifting, conditional on the modularity Hypothesis 4.11;
- Beilinson's ℓ-adic height pairing with local indices;
- R-good primes, admissible sextuples and the normalized height pairing (under LL21 Hypothesis 6.6, or without it when S_π = ∅).

The final theorems:
- Propositions 3.6–3.7 (ramified spherical doubling integral L_σ(s + ½)/b_{2r}(s); L = L_σ, ε = 1);
- Propositions 4.8–4.9;
- Liu's nonsingular Eisenstein expansions;
- Propositions 4.20, 4.27, 4.28, 4.31 (local indices at split, inert, ramified and archimedean places) and Proposition 4.30(4.9);
- Theorem 1.4 (Chow-group nonvanishing, without Hypothesis 4.11);
- Theorem 1.5 (the arithmetic inner product formula, under Hypotheses 4.11 and LL21 6.6);
- Corollary 1.7 (the explicit new-vector formula with (−1)^r);
- Example 1.10 as stated;
- Remarks 4.32–4.33.

Keep F ≠ Q, every 2-adic place split, and every ramified prime unramified over Q. Keep both hypotheses explicit and conditional.

The corresponding paper job for Li–Liu 2021 (#1115) is still open. Its extraction should route into this same Part II rather than a parallel one. Read LL21 §§2–11, Liu 2011, Yamana 2014 and Liu 2022 before closing.

Tests:
- the sign and constant of Corollary 1.7 for r = 1;
- ℨ^♮ = 1 at ramified places off R.

routes.reason:
GN.3 constructs local representation densities. This source adds the ramified hermitian case:
- local densities Den(M, L) against hyperbolic lattices H_s;
- the Cho–Yamauchi-type count (Lemma 2.15, via symplectic isometry counts over the residue field) and the normalized Siegel series with its lattice-count formulas;
- the lattice lemmas the analytic side uses: invariants from minors and type change (Lemma 2.23), the isotropic count (Lemma 2.25), the r = 1 count (Lemma 2.43), and the special lattices and valuation reduction (Lemmas 2.57–2.58).
The normal-basis classification it builds on is planned in GN.2.

MP.2 owns explicit Weil-representation formulas, and MP.3 owns unitary dual pairs and local theta modules with first-occurrence statements. This source adds:
- the Fourier transform and Schrödinger-model formulas for G_r × U(V) over a ramified quadratic extension;
- the spherical module and its annihilator (Proposition 3.4);
- the theta lifting of tempered spherical representations (Proposition 3.9).

IG.5 plans the rational Igusa trace comparison (Caraiani–Scholze), and IG.7 plans localized concentration theorems for unitary Shimura varieties. Theorem 4.21 belongs to that direction: vanishing of Hecke-localized middle cohomology of Drinfeld-level integral models. It uses:
- Newton strata and their Drinfeld-kernel refinements, with Mantovan's smoothness of closures;
- Harris–Taylor Igusa varieties of the first kind;
- the CS17 trace formula with endoscopy, and the Steinberg alternating-sum lemma.
It extends Taylor–Yoshida Proposition 4.4 (Proposition 4.25).
